    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside trio in the outfield that provides both statistical volume and critical stolen base opportunities. **Seiya Suzuki** (73.90) headlines our offensive attack; he draws a favorable matchup against a lefty in Abbott, with his elite SP skill rating and #2 order slot creating a massive ceiling. **Mickey Moniak** (69.47) and **Corbin Carroll** (67.54) also serve as primary drivers today. **Mickey Moniak** benefits specifically from an advantageous platoon split and a slow opposing delivery that screams stolen base upside, while **Corbin Carroll** remains a core tactical start despite a neutral matchup against Sheehan. At 1B, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) earns our highest efficiency score of the day, buoyed by a significant park factor and a favorable platoon advantage against Baz.\n\nWhile we are optimized for efficiency, the bench decision regarding **Kyle Karros** (46.70) was the most difficult pivot. **Kyle Karros** cleared our 40-point threshold comfortably, but ultimately lost out to the superior platoon utility of **Isaac Paredes** (50.46) and the volume upside of our middle infielders. Our biggest risk mitigation strategy today centers on **Brice Turang** (45.98) and **Austin Riley** (49.11); both are hovering near our marginal floor. **Brice Turang** is tasked with navigating the arm of Skenes, where his score is suppressed by a high-end catcher pop-time disadvantage, while **Austin Riley**\u2019s score is dampened by a poor lineup spot at #7 and the defensive capability of the opposing pitcher. We will be watching these two closely, as their inclusion is purely based on the lack of a superior plug-and-play alternative on the bench today.",
